    "evidence_text": "RESULTS The most frequently reported problems involved pain/discomfort (8.8%) and anxiety/depression (7.6%).\n\nNearly half of the respondents who reported problems in any of the five dimensions were chronic patients.\n\nExcept for anemia, all the selected chronic diseases were indicative of a negative EQ VAS score.\n\nThe three leading risk factors were cerebrovascular disease, cancer and mental disease.\n\nIncreases in age, number of chronic conditions and frequency of physical activity were found to have a gradient effect.   \n\n\nObesity as a medical problem.\n\nObesity is now so common within the world's population that it is beginning to replace undernutrition and infectious diseases as the most significant contributor to ill health.\n\nIn particular, obesity is associated with diabetes mellitus, coronary heart disease, certain forms of cancer, and sleep-breathing disorders.\n\nThe global epidemic of obesity results from a combination of genetic susceptibility, increased availability of high-energy foods and decreased requirement for physical activity in modern society.\n\nObesity should no longer be regarded simply as a cosmetic problem affecting certain individuals, but an epidemic that threatens global well being.\n\nBody-mass index and mortality in Korean men and women.\n\nBACKGROUND Obesity is associated with diverse health risks, but the role of body weight as a risk factor for death remains controversial.   \n\n\nThe relative risk of death associated with BMI declined with increasing age.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS Underweight, overweight, and obese men and women had higher rates of death than men and women of normal weight.\n\nThe main outcome measure was CVD mortality across levels of fitness with stratification by BMI.   \n\n\nGeneral and abdominal adiposity and risk of death in Europe.\n\nAfter adjustment for BMI, waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio were strongly associated with the risk of death.\n\nExercise capacity and body mass as predictors of mortality among male veterans with type 2 diabetes.\n\nExercise capacity was determined from a maximal exercise test and measured in metabolic equivalents (METs).\n\nRESULTS During a mean follow-up of 4.8 +/-\n\nBody size and outcomes on peritoneal dialysis in the United States.\n\nBACKGROUND Being overweight is often cited as a relative contraindication to peritoneal dialysis.\n\nOur primary objective was to determine whether actual mortality rates support this opinion.   \n\n\nApart from higher third-year mortality in the obese, associations were similar after censoring at a switch to hemodialysis.\n\nBody mass index and magnetic resonance markers of brain integrity in adults.\n\nThey have also been associated with cognitive dysfunction and brain structural alterations in otherwise healthy adults.\n\nThus, greater BMI may increase the odds of developing an age-related disease, such as Alzheimer's disease.\n\nPsychological Outcome 4 Years after Restrictive Bariatric Surgery\n\nExtreme obesity is associated with severe psychiatric and somatic comorbidity and impairment of psychosocial functioning.\n\nBariatric surgery is the most effective treatment not only with regard to weight loss but also with obesity-associated illnesses.\n\nThese improvements were largely maintained.\n\nHowever, parallel to weight regain, psychological improvements showed a slow but not significant decline over time.\n\nThe Prospective Studies Collaboration aimed to investigate these associations by sharing data from many studies.   \n\n\nThe analyses were adjusted for age, sex, smoking status, and study.\n\nFINDINGS In both sexes, mortality was lowest at about 22.5-25 kg/m(2).\n\nThe progressive excess mortality above this range is due mainly to vascular disease and is probably largely causal.",
